file: "packages/editor/src/shell/chord_label.ts"
doc: "Render a Chord as the text a user reads."

methods: [
  { name: "formatChord"
    change_type: ADDED
    doc:
      "Pure, apart from one platform read described below. Takes a Chord,\n"
      "returns display text. Nothing parses the result back into a Chord.\n"
      "\n"
      "PLATFORM. Read `navigator.platform` and treat a value containing 'Mac'\n"
      "as Apple; ignore userAgent entirely. Read it on every call rather than\n"
      "caching at module scope, so a test can stub it between cases. This\n"
      "matters because resolveChord treats metaKey and ctrlKey as the SAME\n"
      "modifier, so one KEYMAP entry is Cmd on a Mac and Ctrl elsewhere, and\n"
      "a hardcoded symbol is wrong for half the users.\n"
      "\n"
      "COMPOSITION, in this order: meta, then shift, then the key.\n"
      "  On Apple: the symbols U+2318 and U+21E7, concatenated with NO\n"
      "  separator, e.g. Cmd+Shift+Z renders as the two symbols followed\n"
      "  by Z.\n"
      "  Elsewhere: the words Ctrl and Shift, joined to each other and to the\n"
      "  key with '+', e.g. Ctrl+Shift+Z.\n"
      "That is the whole separator rule; there is no other whitespace.\n"
      "\n"
      "THE KEY. Exactly five KeyboardEvent.key values are renamed, and no\n"
      "others: ArrowUp, ArrowDown, ArrowLeft and ArrowRight lose the word\n"
      "Arrow (so 'Up', 'Down', 'Left', 'Right'), and 'Escape' becomes 'Esc'.\n"
      "Every other multi-character key is passed through verbatim --\n"
      "'Backspace' stays 'Backspace', 'Delete' stays 'Delete', 'Enter' stays\n"
      "'Enter'. A single-character key is upper-cased, so 'v' renders 'V' and\n"
      "'?' renders '?'.\n"
      "\n"
      "Backspace is deliberately NOT renamed to Delete even though it is the\n"
      "delete key on Apple keyboards: both are bound to the same command, the\n"
      "help sheet gives each chord its own row, and renaming one would print\n"
      "two rows reading 'Delete' with no way to tell them apart."
    inputs: [ { name: "chord", type: { named: { symbol: "Chord" } } } ]
    output: { primitive: CHARACTER }
  }
]
